Title: "Operator And Member Expressions"
Id: "74b37f13-dca9-4634-90dc-6df6e0b63240"

Text: "MathLingua supports symbolic operators, named operators, infix commands,
ordinary member access, and direct command components.

```mlg-fragment
x * y
x :* y
x *: y
x :*: y
x |op| y
x |M.*| y
x \.set.union./ y
M.X
M.`*`(x, y)
\naturals..N
\naturals..succ(n)
\reals..`*`
```

`x :* y` resolves `*` from the type of `x`; `x *: y` resolves it from the type
of `y`; `x :*: y` resolves from their least common ancestor type. Plain
operators first use a bound local operator as an application, then eligible
capabilities or `Disambiguates:` entries.

Named prefix, infix, and postfix operators behave like function calls. A named
operator may be a dotted member path: `x |M.*| y` is `M.*(x, y)`.

Use one dot for a component of a bound value. Use two dots in source for a
direct component of a top-level `Defines:` or `Realizes:` command. Direct
components are ordinary expressions and may also be specification subjects or
targets, for example `n \"in\" \naturals..N`. The renderer shows a single dot
after semantic resolution. Direct access is not available on a `Declares:`
type."
Id: "3b8fd8f3-9171-44e0-bff4-93872a5afb23"
